{% comment %}
  sections/featured-blog.liquid

  Articles from one blog, as a grid or a carousel.

  ## Two existing implementations, joined rather than copied

  The surface is `sections/main-faq.liquid`'s — colour scheme, background media,
  border, radius, margins and padding, the same setting ids feeding the same
  snippets. The carousel is `sections/featured-collection.liquid`'s, down to the
  last `carousel_*` id, so the arrows, the peek, the pagination and the custom
  colours are one implementation with one set of bugs and one place to fix them.

  Nothing here is a third copy of either.

  ## The card is blocks, not settings

  `snippets/article-card.liquid` still renders the blog index, where every card
  is the same and there is nothing to arrange. A featured row is the other case,
  so the card is `blocks/_article-card.liquid` and everything in it — the
  picture, the pills on it, the read time, the title, the excerpt, the button —
  is a nested block the merchant arranges once.

  It is rendered here rather than added by the merchant, with the same `id` on
  every pass, because a row decides how many cards it has. One id means one set
  of settings shared by all of them: a grid whose third card could be styled
  differently from the first is a grid that will be.

  `closest.article:` is what carries the article into the card *and* into every
  block nested inside it. `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` passes no parameters, so
  a nested Article title has no other way to know which article it is titling.
  `sections/main-product.liquid` passes `closest.product` into its detail blocks
  for exactly the same reason.

  ## Why the carousel element is written out here

  `snippets/carousel-shell.liquid` takes its slides as a captured string, and
  `content_for` output cannot be captured — Shopify renders blocks with their
  editor bindings attached, and buffering that into a variable is not something
  the platform supports. So the element and its track are written here, while
  `carousel-shell-attrs` and `carousel-shell-tail` supply the attributes and the
  controls from the same sub-snippets the shell itself uses.
  `sections/featured-collection.liquid` does the same, for the same reason.

  ## Two answers this section gives that the collection one does not

  **The empty row is drawn, not apologised for.** A blog with no posts renders
  `snippets/article-card-placeholder.liquid` as the slides of the same shell the
  real cards use — one per **Post count**, in whichever layout Type is set to. A
  merchant adds this section before they have written anything more often than
  after, and the theme editor is where the section is judged.

  **View all is a grid-only control.** A carousel already is the way through the
  rest of the blog, so the button is hidden — in the panel and on the page —
  whenever the desktop layout is a carousel. See the note by `show_view_all`.

  ## The settings panel is grouped by what a merchant is deciding

  Blog and alignment, then **Cards layout** — one Type select, the three
  column counts and the two gaps, because those are one decision made once —
  then **Section layout** for the container and View all. Everything a carousel
  needs is under **Carousel**, hidden whole when neither breakpoint is one.

  Type is a single select rather than the Desktop layout / Mobile layout pair it
  replaces. Two selects made four combinations, two of which nobody wants (a
  desktop carousel that becomes a mobile grid is a row a phone cannot finish),
  and the pair could not express Editorial at all — that is a desktop
  arrangement whose mobile answer is always the plain stack. So the desktop
  arrangement is the question, and **Carousel on mobile** is the one bit of
  freedom left underneath it, hidden when Type is already Carousel.

  Its colours are an **Inherit color scheme** checkbox with a colour scheme
  picker under it, rather than the `carousel_color_source` select this section
  used to carry or the **Use custom colors** checkbox
  `sections/featured-collection.liquid` still does.
  `snippets/carousel-style.liquid` reads all three, so the three sections share
  one implementation and none had to be converted for another.

  The checkbox is the same control `blocks/group.liquid` uses for the same
  question, which is the point: a merchant who has turned one off knows what
  this one does. The scheme picker under it sets all eight slider colours at
  once and the pickers below correct one of them, so the common case — "these
  arrows, but on the dark scheme" — is one click rather than eight.

  ### The unparenthesised `visible_if` conditions are not a typo

  A carousel setting that also depends on a toggle reads:

      carousel_navigation and layout == 'carousel' or carousel_on_mobile

  which means *navigation is on **and** either breakpoint is a carousel*.
  `visible_if` is Liquid, and Liquid has no parentheses: operators are evaluated
  right to left, so the `or` binds first and the `and` takes the whole of it.
  Writing the grouping out — `A and (B or C)` — is a `ValidVisibleIf` error,
  which is how this was found rather than guessed at.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings

  assign featured = s.blog
  assign limit = s.articles_to_show | default: 6 | plus: 0

  comment
    Editorial counts in fours — one list card and a row of three — so it brings
    its own count rather than sharing the grid's. Six articles in this layout is
    a cycle and a half: a list card, three across, and a second list card with
    nothing under it. Eight is two whole cycles, which is why it is the default
    and why the setting is its own.

    A merchant who has already set six for a grid does not have that number
    moved out from under them when they try the layout, which one shared setting
    with a switched default would do.
  endcomment
  if s.layout == 'editorial'
    assign limit = s.articles_to_show_editorial | default: 8 | plus: 0
  endif

  assign desktop = s.columns_desktop | default: 3 | plus: 0

  comment
    Mobile columns is a select of two, and it used to be a range of three. A
    theme that ships a narrower control does not narrow the values already
    stored against it: a section saved at three columns keeps the 3, the picker
    has nowhere to show it, and the phone keeps rendering a layout the merchant
    can no longer see they chose. So the stored value is clamped here rather
    than trusted.

    `plus: 0` coerces the select's string to a number first — `"2" > 2` is a
    comparison between a string and an integer, which is false in every Liquid
    and would let a 3 through if the setting were ever widened again.
  endcomment
  assign cols_mobile = s.columns_mobile | default: 1 | plus: 0
  if cols_mobile > 2
    assign cols_mobile = 2
  endif
  if cols_mobile < 1
    assign cols_mobile = 1
  endif

  comment
    `sizes` has to describe the widest a card is ever painted, which is the
    desktop column count. Getting it wrong is the most common cause of a grid
    downloading pictures two or three times larger than it shows.
  endcomment
  assign card_width = 100 | divided_by: desktop
  assign card_sizes = '(min-width: 990px) ' | append: card_width | append: 'vw, (min-width: 750px) 45vw, 90vw'

  comment
    ---- the surface, from `sections/main-faq.liquid` ----
  endcomment
  assign background_media = s.background_media | default: 'none'
  assign has_background_media = false
  if background_media != 'none'
    assign has_background_media = true
  endif

  assign background_paint = ''
  if has_background_media == false
    assign background_paint = s.background_color | default: ''
  endif

  comment
    Opacity goes into the colour rather than onto the element: `opacity` on the
    section would fade the cards along with the frame.

    With no colour picked the border is `currentColor`, which the colour filters
    cannot take an alpha channel from — there is nothing to read until the
    scheme resolves in the browser. `color-mix` does the same fade at that point
    instead, so both paths honour the slider.

    Any style the select offers, not the one value it used to have. Testing
    for `solid` meant the day Dashed was added the width would fall back to
    zero and nothing would be drawn — a new option that silently did nothing.
    `none` is the only value that means no border, so that is what is asked
    about.
  endcomment
  assign border_line = s.border_style | default: 'none'

  comment
    One width per side rather than one for the box. The frame a merchant
    actually wants here is usually a single rule — a hairline across the top
    of the section, nothing down the sides — and a single slider could only
    ever draw all four or none.

    They are summed into `border_any` so the properties are emitted only when
    at least one side is drawn. A zero-width border with a colour on it is
    three declarations that paint nothing.
  endcomment
  assign bw_top = 0
  assign bw_right = 0
  assign bw_bottom = 0
  assign bw_left = 0
  assign border_any = 0

  if border_line != 'none'
    assign bw_top = s.border_width_top | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign bw_right = s.border_width_right | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign bw_bottom = s.border_width_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign bw_left = s.border_width_left | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign border_any = bw_top | plus: bw_right | plus: bw_bottom | plus: bw_left

    assign border_percent = s.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.border_color == blank
      assign border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign border_alpha = border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign border_paint = s.border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  comment
    ---- corners ----

    One radius, twice: the desktop shape and the phone's. Four per-corner
    sliders were the shape before this, and eight controls for a decision that
    is nearly always "round the box by this much" is seven too many.

    A phone gets its own number because the same one does not read the same at
    both widths — 40px is a soft lift across 1400px and a heavy scoop out of a
    390px screen. `| default: radius` rather than a constant, so a section
    saved before the mobile slider existed keeps one shape at every width.
  endcomment
  assign radius = s.border_radius | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign radius_mobile = s.border_radius_mobile | default: radius | plus: 0
  assign margin_top = s.margin_top | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign margin_bottom = s.margin_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the carousel ----

    One `matchMedia` and nothing else when both breakpoints are grids: the
    component checks before it touches Swiper, so a grid-everywhere section
    never constructs one.

    `count` is what lets `carousel-options` decide whether the merchant's Loop
    setting is achievable — Swiper silently disables looping below twice
    `slidesPerView`, which reads as the setting doing nothing.

    Three types, two answers. `grid` and `editorial` are both a CSS grid as far
    as the shell is concerned — editorial is the same grid running a repeating
    magazine pattern, a full-width list card then three across, which `base.css`
    draws off `nth-child(4n + 1)` and mirrors on `8n + 5` — so only `carousel`
    reaches `data-layout`. Anything else here would make the component try to
    build a Swiper out of a layout that is not one.
  endcomment
  assign layout_type = s.layout | default: 'carousel'

  assign editorial_class = ''
  if layout_type == 'editorial'
    assign editorial_class = ' article-grid--editorial'
  endif

  assign cs_layout = 'grid'
  if layout_type == 'carousel'
    assign cs_layout = 'carousel'
  endif

  comment
    `false == blank` is true in Liquid, so a blank check would read "off" as
    "not set" and turn the mobile carousel back on. `nil` is the only value that
    means the merchant has not answered yet.

    A desktop carousel is a carousel on a phone too, whatever this holds — the
    setting is hidden in that case, and a stored `false` from before the type
    was switched must not leave a phone with a six-card stack the merchant
    cannot see they have.
  endcomment
  assign carousel_on_mobile = s.carousel_on_mobile
  if carousel_on_mobile == nil
    assign carousel_on_mobile = true
  endif

  assign cs_layout_mobile = 'grid'
  if layout_type == 'carousel' or carousel_on_mobile
    assign cs_layout_mobile = 'carousel'
  endif

  comment
    ---- the gaps ----

    A Desktop/Mobile pair, published as section-private tokens and mapped onto
    `--grid-gap-x` / `--grid-gap-y` in `base.css` rather than written to those
    two names directly.

    Those two names are still what the whole theme spaces a card grid with —
    `.article-grid` and the shell's pre-init grid both read them, and
    `carousel.js` measures the track's used `column-gap`, which resolves from
    them. So one pair of sliders still moves the grid, the pre-init layout and
    the live carousel together, and a row cannot space itself one way before
    Swiper mounts and another way after.

    What the indirection buys is the Mobile half. A section's settings land in a
    `style` attribute, and an inline custom property beats every rule in every
    stylesheet — so a section that wrote `--grid-gap-x` itself could never be
    stepped down in a media query, at any specificity, and the mobile sliders
    would be controls that move nothing. `carousel.js` re-measures on a
    breakpoint change, so `spaceBetween` follows the step-down too.

    `plus: 0` rather than `default` alone: 0 is a gap a merchant can legitimately
    want, and `default` would replace it. The mobile pair uses an explicit `nil`
    test for the same reason — `default` cannot tell a deliberate 0 from an
    unanswered setting.
  endcomment
  assign gap_x = s.gap_horizontal | default: 24 | plus: 0
  assign gap_y = s.gap_vertical | default: 32 | plus: 0

  assign gap_x_mobile = s.gap_horizontal_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.gap_horizontal_mobile == nil
    assign gap_x_mobile = 12
  endif
  assign gap_y_mobile = s.gap_vertical_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.gap_vertical_mobile == nil
    assign gap_y_mobile = 16
  endif

  assign cs_count = featured.articles_count | default: 0 | plus: 0
  if cs_count > limit
    assign cs_count = limit
  endif

  assign cs_is_carousel = false
  if cs_layout == 'carousel' or cs_layout_mobile == 'carousel'
    assign cs_is_carousel = true
  endif

  comment
    ---- View all ----

    Hidden while the desktop layout is a carousel, and hidden in the same breath
    in the schema. A carousel already *is* the way through the rest of the blog:
    a customer who wants the fourth article drags to it. A button underneath
    saying "view all" alongside a control that already shows all of them is two
    answers to one question, and the second one leaves the page.

    The setting is checked as well as the layout so that turning the row back
    into a grid restores whatever the merchant had chosen, rather than silently
    coming back on.
  endcomment
  assign show_view_all = false
  if s.show_view_all and layout_type != 'carousel' and featured.articles_count > limit
    assign show_view_all = true
  endif

  comment
    ---- The empty state ----

    A row with no articles is drawn as placeholder cards rather than as a line
    of apologetic text. In the theme editor that is the difference between a
    merchant seeing what the section will look like and a merchant seeing a
    sentence, and it is where the section is judged: **Featured blog** is added
    before the first post is written more often than after.

    How many is derived rather than asked for: it is **Post count**, exactly.
    A preview that stops at one row answers the wrong question — the merchant is
    not asking what one row looks like, they are asking what *this section* will
    look like when they have written the posts, and at six posts across three
    columns that is two rows and a different amount of vertical space. Showing
    three of them made the section shorter empty than full, which is the one
    thing a placeholder must not do.

    It also has to be Post count for Editorial to preview at all: that layout is
    a lead card and the cards around it, and a single row of three has no
    "around it" to show.

    Shopify ships three placeholder photographs, so the fourth card repeats the
    first. That is `article-card-placeholder`'s own `modulo`, not something this
    section counts — see the note there on why the pictures vary at all.
  endcomment
  assign has_articles = false
  if featured.articles.size > 0
    assign has_articles = true
  endif

  assign placeholder_count = limit

  comment
    The loop guard counts slides, and when the blog is empty the slides are the
    placeholders. Leaving `cs_count` at zero told `carousel-options` there was
    nothing to loop, so a merchant who had turned Loop on saw it silently
    refused in the one state where they are most likely to be looking at it.
  endcomment
  unless has_articles
    assign cs_count = placeholder_count
  endunless
-%}

{%- capture section_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', section: s, declarations: true -%}
  --fb-gap-x: {{ gap_x }}px;
  --fb-gap-y: {{ gap_y }}px;
  --fb-gap-x-mobile: {{ gap_x_mobile }}px;
  --fb-gap-y-mobile: {{ gap_y_mobile }}px;
  --featured-blog-radius: {{ radius }}px;
  --featured-blog-radius-mobile: {{ radius_mobile }}px;
  --featured-blog-margin-block-start: {{ margin_top }}px;
  --featured-blog-margin-block-end: {{ margin_bottom }}px;
  {%- if border_any > 0 -%}
    --featured-blog-border-width: {{ bw_top }}px {{ bw_right }}px {{ bw_bottom }}px {{ bw_left }}px;
    --featured-blog-border-style: {{ border_line }};
    --featured-blog-border-color: {{ border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if background_paint != '' -%}
    background: {{ background_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="featured-blog color-scheme-container color-{{ s.color_scheme }}{% if has_background_media %} has-background-media{% endif %}"
  data-section-spacing
  {%- if s.stack_layer != blank and s.stack_layer != 'default' %} data-section-layer="{{ s.stack_layer }}"{% endif %}
  data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
  style="{{ section_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
>
  {%- if has_background_media -%}
    {% render 'background-media', settings: s %}
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="{% render 'container', width: s.container_width %}">
    {%- comment -%}
      The merchant's own heading cluster. Whatever they add — a Section header,
      a Text block, a row with a button in it — sits above the cards, and this
      section says nothing about what it should be.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <motion-effect
      class="featured-blog__header featured-blog__header--{{ s.alignment | default: 'start' }}"
      {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', section: s, children: true %}
    >
      {% content_for 'blocks' %}
    </motion-effect>

    {%- comment -%}
      One shell, whichever kind of slide is going into it.

      The empty state used to be a second element — a plain `<ul>` with the
      column counts written on it by hand — on the reasoning that there is
      nothing to page through and arrows over placeholders invite a drag that
      goes nowhere. That reasoning was wrong in the way that matters: a merchant
      who has set Type to Carousel and has not written a post yet was shown a
      two-row grid, and reported the carousel as broken. Correctly — the preview
      is the whole job of the empty state, and it was previewing a layout they
      had not chosen.

      So the placeholders are slides now. The arrows work, the dots work, and
      what the merchant sees before they publish is what they get after. It also
      removes the hand-written `--columns-*` on that second element, which was a
      copy of what `carousel-shell-attrs` already writes and was one edit away
      from disagreeing with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <swiper-carousel
      {% render 'carousel-shell-attrs',
         settings: s,
         layout: cs_layout,
         layout_mobile: cs_layout_mobile,
         classes: 'featured-blog__carousel',
         columns_desktop: s.columns_desktop,
         columns_tablet: s.columns_tablet,
         columns_mobile: cols_mobile,
         count: cs_count %}
    >
      <ul
        class="carousel-shell__track article-grid{{ editorial_class }}{% unless has_articles %} featured-blog__placeholders{% endunless %}"
        data-ref="track"
        role="list"
        {% if has_articles and cs_is_carousel %}aria-label="{{ featured.title | escape }}"{% endif %}
      >
        {%- if has_articles -%}
          {%- for article in featured.articles limit: limit -%}
            <li class="article-grid__item carousel-shell__item" data-article-id="{{ article.id }}">
              {% content_for 'block',
                 type: '_article-card',
                 id: 'article-card',
                 closest.article: article,
                 index: forloop.index0,
                 priority: forloop.first,
                 sizes: card_sizes %}
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- for i in (1..placeholder_count) -%}
            <li class="article-grid__item carousel-shell__item">
              {% render 'article-card-placeholder',
                 index: forloop.index0,
                 columns: desktop,
                 sizes: card_sizes,
                 section: s %}
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      </ul>

      {% render 'carousel-shell-tail',
         settings: s,
         layout: cs_layout,
         layout_mobile: cs_layout_mobile %}
    </swiper-carousel>

    {%- if show_view_all -%}
      <div class="featured-blog__footer">
        {% render 'button', label: 'general.actions.view_all', href: featured.url, variant: 'secondary' %}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.featured_blog.name",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "@theme"
    },
    {
      "type": "@app"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "blog",
      "id": "blog",
      "label": "t:sections.featured_blog.settings.blog.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "alignment",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.label",
      "info": "t:sections.featured_blog.settings.alignment.info",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "start",
          "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "center",
          "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__2.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "end",
          "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.options__3.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "start"
    },

    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:sections.featured_blog.headers.cards"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "layout",
      "label": "t:shared.carousel.layout_type.label",
      "info": "t:shared.carousel.layout_type.info",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "grid",
          "label": "t:shared.carousel.layout_type.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "carousel",
          "label": "t:shared.carousel.layout_type.options__2.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "editorial",
          "label": "t:shared.carousel.layout_type.options__3.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "carousel"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "carousel_on_mobile",
      "label": "t:shared.carousel.carousel_on_mobile.label",
      "info": "t:shared.carousel.carousel_on_mobile.info",
      "default": true,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.layout != 'carousel' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "articles_to_show",
      "label": "t:sections.featured_blog.settings.articles_to_show.label",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 16,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 6,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.layout != 'editorial' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "articles_to_show_editorial",
      "label": "t:sections.featured_blog.settings.articles_to_show.label",
      "info": "t:sections.featured_blog.settings.articles_to_show_editorial.info",
      "min": 4,
      "max": 16,
      "step": 4,
      "default": 8,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.layout == 'editorial' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_desktop",
      "label": "t:shared.columns.desktop.label",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 6,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 3,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.layout != 'editorial' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_tablet",
      "label": "t:shared.carousel.columns_tablet.label",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 4,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "columns_mobile",
      "label": "t:shared.carousel.columns_mobile.label",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "1",
          "label": "t:shared.carousel.columns_mobile.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "2",
          "label": "t:shared.carousel.columns_mobile.options__2.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "1"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "gap_device",
      "label": "t:shared.gap.label",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "desktop",
          "label": "t:shared.spacing.devices.options__1.label"
        },
        {
          "value": "mobile",
          "label": "t:shared.spacing.devices.options__2.label"
        }
      ],
      "default": "desktop"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_horizontal",
      "label": "t:shared.gap.horizontal.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.gap_device == 'desktop' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_vertical",
      "label": "t:shared.gap.vertical.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32,
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.gap_device == 'desktop' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap_horizontal_mobile",
      "label": "t:shared.gap.horizontal.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
